failure canary for the alpha jsPlugins seam, replace-not-merge config authoring, and a never-hand-resolved one-commit reformat (operator pick over the split-lane recommendation) | 2026-08-15 | Tooling |\n | [0409](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0409-oxc-adoption-landed-and-three-corrections-to-0408.md) | The oxc adoption landed — oxlint 1.78 replaces ESLint 10 at rule parity (15s to under 1s; one root config for 72 deleted ones), oxfmt 0.63 reformats 1,636 files in one commit, the mutation-verified jsPlugins canary ships as its own CI step, and three of 0408's factual claims are corrected: eslint/prettier survive as storybook transitive peers (only direct deps and all invocations left), the disable-directive count is 14 not 5, and parity needed five explicit a11y `off`s | 2026-08-16 | Tooling |\n | [0410](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0410-analytics-retirement-delist-and-open-base-reconciliation.md) | `@caisson/analytics` retired — the ADR-0287 wave-1 analytics port never acquired a consumer, so the package is deleted and removed from the served index by an append-only module delist (ADR-0402 mechanics: ledger row, index regenerated, tarball provenance retained); supersedes the ADR-0407 clause parking C01. The external-usage check cleared every channel (npm scope unpublished, OSS mirror still private, registry live but commerce sandbox) and counsel had separately asked for a keep-or-remove decision on this exact package. Open Base goes 17 to 16; `@caisson/ds-manifest` remains served-but-publicly-unnamed and is NOT decided here | 2026-08-18 | Tooling |\n | [0411](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0411-design-critic-retirement-archive-ledger-and-d8-visual-dimension.md) | `tooling/design-critic` retired — its 729-finding ledger is closed (every row `fixed`), so the package is deleted and the ledger archived byte-verbatim at `outputs/archive/audit/design-critic-findings-2026-08-18.toml`; `@caisson/audit-harness` gains a **D8 `visual-quality`** dimension (Nielsen rubric, `gw-frontend-designer` lane, keyed on `apps/*` domains rather than surface class — `apps/admin` is `internal-only` but is still a rendered UI, and `services/*` are `buyer-runtime` but render nothing) as the successor lens. Supersedes the ADR-0134 \"generalized without replacing\" clause and disposes the ADR-0407 C04 parking. The FOLD the audit card proposed is refused: the two stable-ID formulas are hash-space incompatible (three fields vs four), so historical rows are never re-keyed. Tooling workspaces go 8 to 7, Bun workspaces 74 to 73 | 2026-08-19 | Tooling |\n | [0412](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0412-open-base-census-name-ds-manifest-publicly.md) | The public open-Base census is **sixteen**, not fifteen — `@caisson/ds-manifest` is named on every surface that enumerates the set (`BASE_SUBSTRATE_PACKAGES`, the marketing count, the OSS-mirror README, both counsel-facing docs). Completes the ADR-0410 clause that recorded the package as \"served, Apache-2.0, and publicly unnamed… explicitly NOT decided here\": ADR-0410 answered counsel's `analytics` question but did NOT move counsel's arithmetic — site 15 vs registry 16 was unchanged, only the sixteenth package's identity moved (`ds-manifest` first published 2026-07-13 per `registry/ledger.jsonl`, two days after the memo). Both ADR-0410 exits are structurally unavailable: a delist 401s a tarball that `@caisson/cli` + `@caisson/mcp-server` declare in PUBLISHED manifests, and re-licensing commercial trips the open-can't-depend-on-commercial gate twice. Root cause fixed: both public-list guards asserted only `⊆`, so shipped-but-unnamed was invisible to CI by construction — each now asserts the converse, mutation-verified, with a non-vacuity floor | 2026-08-19 | Packaging |\n | [0413](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0413-execute-c13-browser-audit-fold-and-c25-route-derivation.md) | Executes **C13** and **C25**, the third and fourth of ADR-0407's six decision-gated consolidation rows to re-enter through a fresh operator decision. C13: `tooling/browser-audit` — a 39-line status adapter over the shared reconciler with one production caller — folds into the `caisson-production-browser-audit` skill, byte-identical. Its condition (\"a deletion-only patch is invalid\") is the substance: `.agents/` is outside the Bun workspaces, so the deleted manifest was the only turbo-owned build/lint/test home — the skill's tests AND its typecheck now run in the required `check` job (lint and format already covered it; only `tsc` was lost, and adding the leg surfaced four pre-existing zod-4 `z.record` type defects). C25: the visual harness derives its public marketing/legal/writing routes from `lib/routes.ts` and `WRITING_PIECES`, recovering five stale routes and dropping none, with the refuted total-derivation honoured — `/cart`, auth and dashboard stay explicit. Tooling workspaces 7 → 6, Bun workspaces 73 → 72. **C10 and C16 stay parked** | 2026-08-19 | Tooling |\n | [0414](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0414-automate-docs-and-support-bot-deploys-promote-the-python-gate.md) | `caisson-docs` and `caisson-support-bot` join `deploy-railway.yml` — until now **no workflow deployed either service**, and the release train did not either (leg 4 is a dispatch of that same workflow), so the standing \"they deploy only via the release train\" claim in two state docs was false and both drifted. Two steps append after site in the order docs → support-bot (the bot is a client of the docs API). Two defects fixed that would have made it fail live: `timeout-minutes: 25` was EXACTLY docs' `healthcheckTimeout` (1500s), and `railway-deploy.ts` polled a fixed 15 min while docs re-embeds its corpus on every cold boot under a 20-min deadline — so the step would have reported timed-out on a deploy that was still succeeding, taking support-bot down with it (new `--wait-minutes`, default 15 unchanged, docs passes 40). The `support-bot` Python gate is promoted to the **sixth required check** and its `paths:` filter removed in the same commit, sequenced BEFORE the automation because an advisory gate in front of an automatic deploy blocks nothing; `release-readiness.test.ts` now guards the required set itself. Drafted 0413, renumbered at merge per ADR-0088 | 2026-08-19 | Tooling |\n | [0415](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0415-ratify-admin-cloudflare-access-recoupling.md) | PR #448 rewrote `apps/admin/src/proxy.ts` for Cloud Run and resolved **two** configs at module load — `loadOriginGateConfig` and `loadCloudflareAccessConfig`, the latter throwing when `CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN` or `CF_ACCESS_AUD` is absent, with the same fail-closed arming as the origin gate (\"no production value can disable Access\"). The 77-line proxy it replaced had none of them and its header stated the ADR-0283 position outright: \"this app no longer depends on it either way\". So a locked ADR was reversed with no superseding ADR. Nothing broke — both vars were already set on `caisson-admin`/production — but the contradiction was not inert: when the post-merge `deploy-railway` run failed, three sessions burned time on a missing-`CF_ACCESS` hypothesis _because_ source and ADR disagreed (actual cause was unrelated — the origin gate now covers `/healthz`, which `origin-gate.test.ts` asserts returns 403 at the deployed config, and Railway's probe cannot carry the header; tracked separately as CAISSON-208). Ratified rather than reverted: Cloud Run puts admin behind Access, so edge-plus-application is the stronger posture, and the alternative traded a real control for documentary tidiness. ADR-0283's better-auth session gate is **unchanged and still the authorization mechanism** — Access is a second, earlier gate; only 0283's no-dependency claim retires, and \"gate-drop-second\" is no longer a standing plan. Consequence: the Access pair is an operational startup requirement, invisible to a repo grep | 2026-08-25 | Architecture |\n | [0417](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0417-publish-the-registry-index-digest-on-both-health-probes.md) | Both health probes publish `indexDigest` + `indexEntries` to every caller. The origin gate they were conditioned on proves **provenance** (the request arrived through our Worker), never **authentication** — and the Worker injects the secret into all edge traffic, so measured 2026-09-01 both surfaces already served the digest to the whole internet while withholding it only from a caller who bypassed the front door and could obtain it by using it. Supersedes ADR-0416 ruling 1's status-only half; the path carve itself stays. The accepted disclosure is a change-fingerprint plus a catalog count — NOT derivable from the public listing, which is entitlement-filtered to 16 of the index's 52 entries. |\n | [0418](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0418-session-hint-cookie-goes-httponly.md) | `caisson_sess_hint` becomes `HttpOnly`; the owned-items fetch always fires and `GET /api/cart/owned` short-circuits server-side on the cookie's ABSENCE, before any session resolution. Trades one cheap request per signed-out page view for removing a client-readable auth-state signal. Also the real citation for a cookie whose three code comments cited ADR-0315 — the close-out triage picker, which says nothing about it. |\n | [0419](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0419-pin-the-image-publish-scan-posture-with-a-local-guard.md) | A test in `tooling/audit-harness` (landing in PR #470) pins the image-publish scan's non-blocking posture: the template's unset-variable fallback to zero, the absence of a local override, and the playbook section that records the choice. `publish-image.yml` is a byte-identical template copy and is never edited — the guard exists precisely because the default lives in someone else's file. |\n | [0420](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0420-schedule-the-subprocess-environment-default-flip.md) | `@caisson/agent-kernel` and `@caisson/tool-exec` gain an opt-in per-command child environment; the inherit-everything default stays for one more release cycle and the flip to narrow is scheduled as a **major** with migration notes. The current default is pinned by a test asserting a child DOES see a parent canary, so the flip cannot land silently. |\n | [0421](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0421-caisson-internal-use-grant.md) | Internal GridWork use of any `@caisson/*` package is granted, commercial included (operator ruling Round 2 #13): it does not pass through the Paddle catalog, changes no `license` field or catalog membership, and does not extend to anything shipped to a third party. Names its five subjects so the grant is greppable — `ai-evals`, `alerting`, `audit-worm`, `observability`, `rate-limit`. The **distribution** half is answered separately and is **blocked**: GitHub Packages is out estate-wide and SPEC D3's vendor-by-copy is proven only on single-file configs, while `audit-worm` is 66 files with three workspace-internal deps. Scopes the gridwork-core authority-ledger dogfood without authorizing it — no `@caisson/*` import lands there until distribution is answered |\n | [0422](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0422-unify-bun-fleet-and-smoke-next-build-images.md) | Unifies all Bun pins on 1.4.2 with image index digests, retiring PR #478's temporary build/runtime split after the September 10–12 Next build outage. Adds path-gated demos/site Docker build smoke with CI mutation evidence and records the Next typegen regeneration; a Bun regression in the Next build path reopens the decision. |\n+| [0423](../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0423-bind-tool-approvals-to-private-records.md) | R359 locks private digest-bound, one-shot tool approval records, current-policy revalidation and spec-owned child environment; supersedes ADR-0360 S3 caller-owned execution proposals. |\n \n ---\n \n ## Accepted is not the same as shipped\n \n An ADR status records a decision, never implementation or deployment. Use\n [`docs/build-state.md`](build-state.md) for repository implementation,\n [`docs/state/production-readiness.md`](state/production-readiness.md) for launch state, and\n [`docs/deploy/STATE.md`](deploy/STATE.md) for runtime evidence. This index is only the append-only\n decision catalog; no row here proves that code, production, or a release is complete.\n \n ## Accuracy flags\n \n - **Stale \"proposed\" headers.** ADRs 0001-0019 and 0024 still read `Status: proposed` in their\n   own header text (written at the Phase-5 spec, pending \"Gate 4\"). The board\n   (`docs/state/decisions-and-forks.md` line 90) treats the whole 0001-0024 range as locked / in\n   force. This index prints the literal header value and flags the gap here rather than silently\n   promoting them.\n - **ADR-0024 internal renumber note** in its own header reads \"Renumbered 0020->0023\"; the file\n   is on disk as `ADR-0024`. Treated as a stale drafting artifact; its substance (amends 0007\ndiff --git a/docs/state/decisions-and-forks.md b/docs/state/decisions-and-forks.md\nindex e0efcb04..8e652434 100644\n--- a/docs/state/decisions-and-forks.md\n+++ b/docs/state/decisions-and-forks.md\n@@ -1,38 +1,41 @@\n ---\n updated: 2026-09-05\n status: live\n-adr_ceiling: 0422\n+adr_ceiling: 0423\n ---\n \n # Decisions & Forks — live board\n \n The single live board (CLAUDE.md source-of-truth #1). Locked → an ADR; open → waits for the\n operator. Never auto-decide a fork.\n \n **Execution status (2026-07-25):** the ADR-0383/0384/0386 OSCAL wave is complete on\n `feature/oscal-spine-wave`, superseding the “not started” execution note preserved in the lock\n narrative below. The operative catalog is Compliance $1,649, Everything $2,259, and\n `oscal-spine` $249; the amended merge point is before the first release train.\n \n ## Locked (→ ADRs / specs)\n \n+R359 / EST-ASK-317 locks digest-bound private tool approval records, current-policy\n+revalidation and spec-owned environment: [ADR-0423](../../knowledge/decisions/ADR-0423-bind-tool-approvals-to-private-records.md).\n+\n | #                                                                       | Decision                                       